From 645838b6c62308c8cc5d32ae937bb992e5c229f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ethan Lee <ethantlee21@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 11:37:53 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] feat: report the foreground process group to a client that
 asks, so that a frontend follows a remote session without polling the host

A GUI frontend over a remote session (Macterm) names the tab after the
foreground process, warns before it closes a pane whose program still
runs, and shows a logo for a coding agent. Until now it asked the host
over a separate ssh connection every few seconds, and the answer was a
round trip old: when an agent quit, the shell's prompt title arrived
while the last answer still said the agent was in front, so the title
had to be held until a later probe confirmed it (thdxg/macterm#695).

The daemon now reads the pty's foreground process group before it
forwards each chunk of output, and every 500 ms while a client
subscribed, and sends a `Foreground` message to each subscriber when it
changes. The client writes the report into its stdout as an APC
sequence, in front of the output it precedes, so a report that the
shell is back always lands before the shell's prompt. APC because no
keyboard produces one, a terminal that does not know it discards it,
and it rides the client's own connection.

Only a client started with `ZMX_FOREGROUND_EVENTS` in its environment
subscribes. It removes the variable before the session starts, so a
shell in the session does not inherit it: a nested `zmx attach` would
otherwise report its own foreground into the outer session. An old
daemon ignores the tag, and an old client ignores the report, so the
two versions mix.

The report carries the host's verdicts (the foreground is the session's
own shell; the name is in /etc/shells), the process name and the
command line, with control bytes replaced and the line cut, so the
sequence always ends where the client ends it. The name comes from
proc_pidpath on macOS and /proc on Linux; on any other platform the
daemon never reports, and the frontend keeps its old way.

The tag is 24, after the downstream Claim.
